1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,264
So far, a word was just a slot:

2
00:00:03,264 --> 00:00:06,064
one entry in a list of 50,000.

3
00:00:06,314 --> 00:00:08,691
The slot only marks which word it is:

4
00:00:08,691 --> 00:00:12,063
nothing in tiger's slot says
it's an animal, like owl.

5
00:00:12,363 --> 00:00:14,752
So the field gave every word a profile:

6
00:00:14,752 --> 00:00:17,240
scores on a few traits, like how much

7
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:19,710
it's about software, or about animals.

8
00:00:19,860 --> 00:00:21,703
Each bar is just a number.

9
00:00:21,903 --> 00:00:23,428
And those numbers are weights,

10
00:00:23,428 --> 00:00:25,623
like the votes from last lesson.

11
00:00:25,823 --> 00:00:28,176
In real models, nobody writes them,

12
00:00:28,176 --> 00:00:29,743
or even picks the traits.

13
00:00:29,743 --> 00:00:32,059
They start out random, and the network

14
00:00:32,059 --> 00:00:34,471
learns them by itself, from text.

15
00:00:34,721 --> 00:00:36,156
That learned row of numbers

16
00:00:36,156 --> 00:00:38,057
is called the word's embedding.

17
00:00:41,484 --> 00:00:43,247
Here's a map of these embeddings,

18
00:00:43,247 --> 00:00:46,241
from a network
nobody told what words mean.

19
00:00:46,241 --> 00:00:48,404
No labels, no definitions.

20
00:00:48,604 --> 00:00:51,898
It only ever played one game:
guess the missing word.

21
00:00:52,198 --> 00:00:54,957
Yet tiger and owl ended up side by side,

22
00:00:54,957 --> 00:00:58,115
even if they never appeared
in the same sentence.

23
00:00:58,415 --> 00:01:01,500
By the end, you'll know
how a guessing game builds this map,

24
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:03,138
and what the map buys you.

25
00:01:06,288 --> 00:01:08,109
What is a word, to a network?

26
00:01:08,909 --> 00:01:11,251
Last time, every word cast two votes:

27
00:01:11,251 --> 00:01:12,995
one for the animal neuron,

28
00:01:12,995 --> 00:01:15,018
and one for the software neuron.

29
00:01:15,018 --> 00:01:19,867
Not just yes or no: each vote
has a strength, called its weight.

30
00:01:19,967 --> 00:01:22,021
Those two weights are just two numbers,

31
00:01:22,071 --> 00:01:24,468
so we can plot every embedding on a map.

32
00:01:24,618 --> 00:01:30,621
Words that act alike sit close together:
tiger next to owl, csv far across the map.

33
00:01:30,821 --> 00:01:33,877
That's an embedding:
a profile of numbers for each word.

34
00:01:33,877 --> 00:01:37,143
It's just each word's votes
for the first neurons.

35
00:01:37,443 --> 00:01:39,952
But lesson 2 drew this map from labels:

36
00:01:39,952 --> 00:01:42,830
we told it which sentences
were about animals.

37
00:01:42,830 --> 00:01:45,003
Real text has no labels.

38
00:01:45,810 --> 00:01:46,874
So without labels,

39
00:01:46,874 --> 00:01:48,890
where do the profiles come from?

40
00:01:49,590 --> 00:01:51,133
The field's big discovery:

41
00:01:51,133 --> 00:01:52,848
text can label itself.

42
00:01:52,848 --> 00:01:54,991
Hide a word, and have the network

43
00:01:54,991 --> 00:01:56,420
guess it from its neighbors.

44
00:01:56,420 --> 00:01:57,992
The hidden word is the answer,

45
00:01:57,992 --> 00:02:00,435
so any text becomes a lesson.

46
00:02:00,585 --> 00:02:03,651
The neighbors' profiles point
to a spot on the map,

47
00:02:03,651 --> 00:02:05,853
and the guess is the word closest to it.

48
00:02:05,853 --> 00:02:10,465
On a wrong guess, backpropagation pulls
the right word toward that spot,

49
00:02:10,465 --> 00:02:12,348
like the votes last time.

50
00:02:12,548 --> 00:02:15,712
Here's the trick.
Tiger and owl never share a sentence.

51
00:02:15,712 --> 00:02:17,825
But they fill the same blanks.

52
00:02:18,329 --> 00:02:20,405
The words start scattered at random.

53
00:02:20,405 --> 00:02:22,020
After thousands of rounds,

54
00:02:22,020 --> 00:02:24,567
owl ends up right next to tiger.

55
00:02:24,767 --> 00:02:28,641
Same blanks, same nudges, same place.

56
00:02:29,641 --> 00:02:31,606
Each word gets only two numbers,

57
00:02:31,606 --> 00:02:34,640
so it can't keep a private answer
for every blank.

58
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:37,457
Words that fill the same blanks
have to share.

59
00:02:37,607 --> 00:02:40,543
A linguist put it this way in 1957:

60
00:02:40,543 --> 00:02:43,333
“You shall know a word
by the company it keeps.”

61
00:02:43,633 --> 00:02:46,095
And notice: the map's sides have no names.

62
00:02:46,095 --> 00:02:50,150
Nobody named the traits;
only the neighborhoods mean something.

63
00:02:50,957 --> 00:02:52,650
So what do you get for it?

64
00:02:53,350 --> 00:02:58,606
Teach it a task with just two labels:
tiger is an animal, csv is software.

65
00:02:58,706 --> 00:03:01,175
One neuron reads each word's profile,

66
00:03:01,175 --> 00:03:05,327
and one neuron makes one straight cut:
a line across the map.

67
00:03:05,477 --> 00:03:08,248
Your turn:
it was only taught tiger and csv.

68
00:03:08,248 --> 00:03:10,115
What does it say about owl?

69
00:03:12,415 --> 00:03:15,023
Animal.
Owl sits on tiger's side of the line,

70
00:03:15,023 --> 00:03:17,287
and so does every animal near it.

71
00:03:17,437 --> 00:03:19,993
Last lesson, a rule was a vote per word.

72
00:03:19,993 --> 00:03:22,230
Now a rule is a cut through the map:

73
00:03:22,230 --> 00:03:25,466
teach one word,
and its whole neighborhood learns.

74
00:03:25,666 --> 00:03:29,453
That's why language models first
read mountains of raw text.

75
00:03:29,453 --> 00:03:33,909
The guessing game builds the map;
then a few examples go a long way.

76
00:03:34,609 --> 00:03:36,660
Our map has two numbers per word.

77
00:03:36,660 --> 00:03:42,510
Real models use hundreds, even thousands,
because words differ in many ways at once.

78
00:03:42,660 --> 00:03:45,506
You can't draw that map,
but closeness still works.

79
00:03:45,506 --> 00:03:48,125
In real profiles with 100 numbers,

80
00:03:48,125 --> 00:03:51,436
Tuesday and Wednesday
line up almost perfectly.

81
00:03:51,586 --> 00:03:54,657
Tuesday's nearest neighbors
are the other weekdays.

82
00:03:54,657 --> 00:03:58,530
Tiger's are tigers, leopard,
elephant, and woods.

83
00:03:59,361 --> 00:04:00,481
That woods is a clue.

84
00:04:00,481 --> 00:04:02,087
Each word gets one place,

85
00:04:02,087 --> 00:04:05,470
so a word with two meanings
gets stuck in between.

86
00:04:05,620 --> 00:04:08,510
Our pandas fills zoo blanks
and software blanks,

87
00:04:08,510 --> 00:04:10,770
and lands between the two neighborhoods.

88
00:04:10,770 --> 00:04:13,893
Real profiles do it too:
python sits next to

89
00:04:13,893 --> 00:04:17,383
monty and cleese,
and next to php and perl.

90
00:04:17,583 --> 00:04:19,918
The words around it still have to decide.

91
00:04:19,918 --> 00:04:22,285
Next: a neuron nobody asked for.

92
00:04:22,285 --> 00:04:26,508
How can a model come up
with complex concepts on its own?
